-- title:
--   Definitions for Schwartz operators
-- statement:
--   The objects the formalized statements of *Schwartz operators* (arXiv:1503.04086) are stated in. Each was checked against the paper by a blind read-back and a moderator.
--
--   - `CanonicalHilbert`: The canonical Hilbert space consists of L2 equivalence classes, PDF p.3.
--   - `Schwartz`: Smooth rapidly decreasing functions with the full Schwartz topology, PDF p.4.
--   - `CanonicalOperator`: Bounded complex-linear operators on the full canonical Hilbert space, PDF p.3.
--   - `MultiIndex`: Nonnegative multi-indices, including the zero index, PDF p.4.
--   - `schwartzToHilbert`: The actual Schwartz-to-L2 inclusion; no representative is chosen, PDF p.4.
--   - `IsSchwartzVector`: Existence of a genuine Schwartz representative of an L2 class, PDF p.4.
--   - `position`: Position restricted to Schwartz functions, multiplication by q_i, PDF p.3.
--   - `momentum`: Momentum restricted to Schwartz functions, -i times the directional derivative, PDF p.3. The standard real coordinate vector is Pi.single i 1.
--   - `multiAction`: Ordered coordinate powers A_1^a_1 ... A_n^a_n on Schwartz functions, PDF p.4, (10); the rightmost operator acts first.
--   - `testAction`: The order P^b Q^a in the test vector of (11) and (18), PDF pp.4,11.
--   - `vectorAction`: The order Q^a P^b in the vector seminorm (9), PDF p.4.
--   - `vectorFormValues`: Values on the Schwartz unit ball in (11), PDF p.4.
--   - `vectorFormBound`: The supremum in (11). Statements always supply boundedness explicitly; unbounded sets are never interpreted as having finite real supremum.
--   - `operatorFormValues`: Values of the four-index sesquilinear form on the two unit balls, (18), PDF p.11. The two primed indices are the right indices.
--   - `operatorFormBound`: The form seminorm, PDF p.11, (18); used on the bounded-form domain.
--   - `IsSchwartzOperator`: Definition 3.1, PDF p.11: CanonicalHilbert boundedness of every form. This does not assume the range theorem, a Schwartz kernel, or a Frechet topology.
--   - `schwartzSubmodule`: The complex vector space underlying Definition 3.1, PDF pp.11--12. Only closure under the vector operations is built here; no topological or completeness property is built into this submodule.
--   - `SchwartzOperator`: Schwartz operators as a vector space, with topology defined separately, PDF p.11. The inherited operator norm topology is never used for source claims.
--   - `schwartzOperatorTopology`: The original topology generated by all translated form-seminorm balls, PDF pp.11--12. Centers range over Schwartz operators; ambient operator norm is not substituted for the Schwartz topology.
--   - `oneSidedTopology`: The topology generated by just the left and right one-sided form seminorms, PDF p.17. Equality with the original topology is a conclusion of Theorem 3.12.
--   - `CanonicalPolynomial`: Noncommutative polynomial expressions in the canonical generators, PDF p.12. Each term is a coefficient and an ordered word; redundant expressions are permitted, and every source polynomial has such a finite presentation.
--   - `wordAction`: Canonical words acting on Schwartz functions, PDF pp.3,12.
--   - `canonicalAction`: Evaluation of a canonical polynomial on Schwartz functions, PDF p.12.
--   - `canonicalAdjoint`: Formal adjoint on the common Schwartz domain, PDF pp.3,12: conjugate coefficients and reverse word order; Q_i and P_i are symmetric there.
--   - `polynomialFormValues`: Weak form of f_L T f_R on Schwartz unit balls, PDF p.12.
--   - `polynomialTopology`: Topology generated by translated polynomial-form seminorm balls, PDF p.12. Proposition 3.4 identifies these finite bounds with actual extension norms.
--   - `rangeSeminormValues`: Values of the supremum of vector seminorms over the full CanonicalHilbert unit ball, PDF p.17. Finiteness is asserted before using its real supremum.
--   - `positiveFrameTrace`: The usual extended trace of a positive bounded operator: supremum of its finite orthonormal diagonal sums, PDF p.7 (the trace used in Schatten norms). Only positive operators are passed to this definition in the ideal predicates. The ENNReal codomain retains infinity and avoids a default-valued real sum.
--   - `IsSchatten`: Schatten p membership for positive integer p, Tr(|T|^p) finite, PDF p.7. The absolute value and positive square root use the actual operator CFC.
--   - `integerSchattenNorm`: The p-norm as an extended nonnegative real, PDF p.7. Used only for p >= 1; on the Schatten class this is exactly the ordinary finite norm.
--   - `finiteSchwartzSum`: Finite sums of Schwartz rank-one operators, PDF p.7, (16).
--   - `ClosedOperatorPolynomial`: Finite expressions in closed operators with scalar constants, sums, scalar multiplication and ordered products, Lemma 3.9, PDF p.15. Closedness is required only of the leaves, never of an arbitrary product.
--   - `closedPolynomialGraph`: Graph semantics of a polynomial on its natural domain, PDF p.15. Sums require both summands to exist; pq requires qx to be in dom(p). No zero-times-undefined or cancellation extension is made.
--   - `CanonicalPhase`: Phase coordinates (q,p), conceptually distinct from double configuration, PDF pp.2--3,7.
--   - `phaseMeasure`: The source's scaled phase measure dx=(2 pi)^(-n) dq dp, PDF p.3.
--   - `vacuumWave`: The n-dimensional Gaussian ground-state wavefunction, PDF pp.5,22. The one-dimensional h_0 in the prose is tensored over all modes.
--   - `rankOneKernel`: Kernel of the actual rank-one dyad |psi><phi|, PDF pp.9,21.
--   - `rankOneCharacteristic`: Tr(W(q,p)|psi><phi|)=<phi,W(q,p)psi>, directly using (2),(4), PDF p.3. Intended for Schwartz wavefunctions, including vacuumWave.
--   - `rankOneWigner`: Source-normalized rank-one Wigner function, PDF p.21: the integral has no (2 pi)^(-n) factor; that factor belongs to phaseMeasure.
--   - `scaledSymbol`: Scaled symbols epsilon^(-2n) g(x/epsilon), PDF p.38; epsilon>0 in every use.
--   - `PositiveScale`: Positive dilation parameters, tending to zero from the right, PDF p.38.
--   - `scalesToZero`: The epsilon->0+ filter on positive scale parameters, PDF p.38.
